          body: `One strong way to use gematria is to avoid treating every isolated match as equally meaningful. Start by gathering words that share a root concept, a scriptural topic, or a repeated symbol. Once those terms sit beside each other, the totals become easier to interpret because you can see whether a pattern repeats inside a real theme instead of appearing once by chance.\n\nAfter that first pass, note which totals recur across categories. Ask whether the same number appears in action words, names, or descriptive terms. A repeated total can be useful, but the surrounding idea matters just as much. When several related words cluster around one value, you have a better basis for further study than you would from a single surprising result.\n\nA practical workflow is simple: calculate a lead term, open the database, collect related words, and then write short notes about why each result belongs in the group. That turns a quick lookup into a reusable research trail.`,
