          summary: 'Use this 10-step checklist to test patterns, reduce bias, and build stronger gematria conclusions from themed word sets.',
          imageUrl: '/assets/blog-beginners-checklist-hero-16x9.svg',
          sliderImageUrl: '/assets/blog-beginners-checklist-slider-21x9.svg',
          body: `<p>Gematria can produce surprising matches fast. That is exactly why beginners need a checklist. Without a process, it is easy to treat one interesting number as proof of a larger meaning. With a process, you can separate useful signal from random overlap.</p><p>Start by writing one clear research question, then lock your input rules before you calculate anything: spelling, punctuation, abbreviations, and transliteration choices. If those rules shift mid-study, your comparisons drift and your confidence drops.</p><p>Next, build a themed set of terms instead of chasing isolated words. Record all outcomes, including non-matches, so you can judge whether a pattern is truly recurring or selectively collected. Check neighboring values, test at least one alternate explanation, and separate your observations from your interpretation notes.</p><p>Before publishing a conclusion, test variant sensitivity and assign a confidence label (low, moderate, high) based on how stable your pattern remains after controls. Every study should end with one concrete next step: expand the set, run a control set, or verify variants.</p><p><strong>Quick checklist:</strong></p><ul><li>Define one research question.</li><li>Lock spelling and formatting rules.</li><li>Use a themed word set.</li><li>Record matches and non-matches.</li><li>Check nearby value clusters.</li><li>Test an alternative explanation.</li><li>Separate data from interpretation.</li><li>Check variant/transliteration sensitivity.</li><li>Assign confidence honestly.</li><li>Set one next verification step.</li></ul><p>If you cannot check most of these boxes yet, the right move is another research pass—not a final conclusion.</p>`,
